WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
How do you test when experience wires a developing brain?

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.440
Close one eye briefly, then listen to cortex.

3
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.980
Sensory deprivation alters input at a chosen age to watch circuits rewire.

4
00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:22.207
Start in primary visual cortex, the first cortical stop for signals from the eyes.

5
00:00:23.527 --> 00:00:26.887
In its binocular zone, most neurons answer both eyes.

6
00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:32.187
Contralateral means opposite side; ipsilateral means same side.

7
00:00:33.527 --> 00:00:36.727
Most mouse cells answer the contralateral eye more strongly.

8
00:00:37.917 --> 00:00:43.417
Score preference with the ocular dominance index: contralateral minus ipsilateral,

9
00:00:43.697 --> 00:00:44.997
divided by their sum.

10
00:00:46.157 --> 00:00:51.597
An illustrative cell fires 30 spikes a second to contralateral input, 10 to

11
00:00:51.597 --> 00:00:56.997
ipsilateral. 30 − 10 is 20; 30 + 10 is 40.

12
00:00:58.357 --> 00:01:06.457
20 ÷ 40 is 0.50. +1 is contralateral only; minus −1, ipsilateral only.

13
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.420
Now suture the contralateral eyelid shut, letting only diffuse light enter.

14
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.400
This is monocular deprivation.

15
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.760
At postnatal day 28, about four days of closure weaken that eye's responses.

16
00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:27.100
The index drops and can fall below zero, favoring the open eye.

17
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.120
Change input at one age, and measure what rewires.

18
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:37.990
Yet closing one eye fits two ideas: simple disuse, or competition between eyes.

19
00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:44.650
In an illustrative model, give an open eye 10 units of useful activity and a sutured

20
00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:49.950
eye 1. If you close both eyes instead, what does each idea predict?

21
00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.700
Under disuse, a sutured eye keeps 1 of 10 units, 10%, either way.

22
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.940
Under competition, its share is its activity divided by the sum.

23
00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.200
With one eye closed, 1 ÷ 11 is about 9%.

24
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:17.600
With both closed, 1 ÷ 2 stays 50%, separating the ideas.

25
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.730
Another check locates the change.

26
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:26.710
The closed eye's retina and LGN, its relay to cortex, still respond.

27
00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:31.750
Test adults too, and sample the whole age curve, not one number.

28
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:37.010
In this film's terms, a critical period shuts abruptly like a door.

29
00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.970
A sensitive period fades gradually, like a tide going out.

30
00:02:42.983 --> 00:02:48.783
In 1963, Wiesel and Hubel closed one kitten eye near birth for about three months.

31
00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:55.543
Very few cortical cells answered the closed eye, yet its retina and LGN still

32
00:02:55.543 --> 00:02:59.043
responded. An adult cat showed no change.

33
00:03:00.243 --> 00:03:05.263
In infant macaques, Hubel, Wiesel and Simon LeVay traced each eye's stripes of

34
00:03:05.263 --> 00:03:06.823
territory in layer 4C.

35
00:03:07.723 --> 00:03:10.923
Open-eye stripes widened; closed-eye stripes narrowed.

36
00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:17.927
In 1996, Gordon and Stryker varied the age of monocular deprivation in mice.

37
00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:25.647
The shift was largest around day 28, much smaller before about day 19 or after about

38
00:03:25.647 --> 00:03:31.867
day 32. Those ages hold for mouse visual cortex, not for song or language.

39
00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:38.367
Changing input across ages, with controls, reveals when experience wires circuits.
